// Unit 2 "Stocks", Chapter 1: What a Stock Really Is
export default {
  id: 'stocks-ch1',
  title: 'What a Stock Really Is',
  blurb: 'Shares, IPOs, market caps, sectors and splits: what you actually own when you own a stock, and where the returns come from.',
  lessons: [
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1
    {
      id: 'stocks-1-what-is-a-share',
      title: 'What You Actually Own',
      summary: 'A share is a slice of a real business. Learn what that slice gets you: profits, assets, votes, and why not all shares are created equal.',
      minutes: 13,
      icon: 'briefcase',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'A slice of the bakery', html: `<p>Ms. Ortiz's bakery is booming. To open a second location she turns the business into a corporation, <strong>Ortiz Bakeries Inc.</strong>, and splits ownership into <strong>1,000 shares</strong>.</p><p>Maya, who has been saving from her café job, buys <strong>10 shares</strong>. That makes her the owner of <span class="hl">10 / 1,000 = 1%</span> of the company. Not 1% of the croissants. 1% of the <em>business</em>: its ovens, its recipes, its lease, its debts, and its future profits.</p><p>That is all a <span class="term" data-def="A unit of ownership in a corporation. Also called a share or equity.">stock</span> is. When you buy a share of a giant company, the math is identical. The company just has billions of shares instead of a thousand, so your slice is microscopic. Microscopic, but real.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: "Congrats, Maya. You are now a part-owner of a bakery. Please do not try to collect your 1% of the muffins." } },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'fact', title: 'Under 18?', html: `<p>In the US you generally must be 18 to open your own brokerage account. Teens like Maya usually invest through a <span class="term" data-def="A brokerage account an adult manages for a minor. The assets legally belong to the minor.">custodial account</span> that a parent or guardian opens and manages until the minor comes of age. The ownership math in this lesson works exactly the same.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'What the slice gets you', html: `<p>Owning a share gives you three main things:</p><ul><li><strong>A claim on profits.</strong> If the bakery earns money, part of it is economically yours. The board decides whether to pay it out as a <span class="term" data-def="A cash payment a company makes to its shareholders out of profits.">dividend</span> or reinvest it to grow. Either way, it belongs to the owners.</li><li><strong>A claim on assets.</strong> If the company ever shuts down and sells everything, shareholders get what is left <em>after</em> everyone else is paid.</li><li><strong>A vote.</strong> Usually one vote per share on big decisions, like who sits on the board of directors.</li></ul><p>You also get <span class="term" data-def="Shareholders can lose what they paid for their shares but are not personally on the hook for the company's debts.">limited liability</span>. If Ortiz Bakeries goes bust owing the flour supplier $50,000, the supplier cannot come after Maya's savings. The most she can lose is what she paid for her shares.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Last in line', html: `<p>That claim on assets has a catch. Shareholders are the <span class="hl">residual claimants</span>: they get whatever is left over. In a liquidation, the line usually looks like this:</p><ol><li>Secured lenders (like a bank with a loan backed by the ovens)</li><li>Other creditors: suppliers, bondholders, unpaid wages and taxes are in here too</li><li>Preferred shareholders</li><li>Common shareholders, last</li></ol><p>In many bankruptcies, common shareholders get nothing at all. That is the price of the upside: lenders get a fixed payment and go first, owners get everything above that and go last. If the bakery triples its profits, the bank still only gets its interest. Maya gets the growth.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Owners eat last. When a company fails, common stock can go to zero while the lenders still get paid. That is the deal you sign up for." } },
        { type: 'check', question: "Ortiz Bakeries goes bankrupt. After selling everything, there is only enough cash to pay the bank loan and half of what suppliers are owed. What do common shareholders get?", options: ['Nothing', 'Their original investment back', '1% each of whatever remains', 'The same as suppliers, split evenly'], answer: 0, explain: 'Common shareholders are paid only after every creditor is paid in full. If creditors are not fully covered, common stock gets nothing.' },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'myth', title: 'Myth: Buying a stock sends money to the company', html: `<p>When Leo buys shares through his brokerage app, his money goes to <strong>another investor</strong> who is selling, not to the company. The company only raises money when it <em>issues</em> new shares, like in an IPO or a follow-on offering. After that, shares mostly trade between investors on the <span class="term" data-def="The market where already-issued shares trade between investors, such as on the NYSE or Nasdaq.">secondary market</span>.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Your vote, such as it is', html: `<p>Shareholders elect the <span class="term" data-def="A group elected by shareholders to oversee management, hire and fire the CEO, and approve big decisions like dividends.">board of directors</span>, who hire the CEO and keep an eye on management. Owners also vote on things like mergers, executive pay (an advisory "say on pay" vote in the US), and proposals other shareholders put forward.</p><p>Most votes happen around the <strong>annual meeting</strong>. Hardly anyone shows up in person. Instead, you get a <span class="term" data-def="A document and ballot that lets you vote your shares without attending the meeting.">proxy</span> and vote online, or let it go unvoted.</p><p>Is Maya's 10-share vote going to swing anything? No. But votes add up. Big index funds and pension funds hold huge blocks of shares, and their votes can decide a close fight.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "Fun fact: a proxy fight is when an outside investor campaigns for shareholder votes to replace board members. It is politics, with spreadsheets." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Common vs preferred', html: `<p>Most shares you can buy are <strong>common stock</strong>: full upside, votes, last in line. Some companies also issue <strong>preferred stock</strong>, which is a bit of a hybrid between a stock and a bond.</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Common</th><th>Preferred</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dividend</td><td>Variable, can be zero</td><td>Usually a fixed rate, paid first</td></tr><tr><td>Upside</td><td>Unlimited</td><td>Mostly capped</td></tr><tr><td>Vote</td><td>Usually yes</td><td>Usually no</td></tr><tr><td>In liquidation</td><td>Last</td><td>Ahead of common</td></tr></tbody></table><p>If a preferred dividend is <span class="term" data-def="Missed preferred dividends pile up and must be paid before common shareholders can get any dividend.">cumulative</span>, any skipped payments must be caught up before common holders see a cent. Preferred holders trade upside for a steadier, higher-priority income stream.</p>` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each feature to the type of stock it best describes.', pairs: [ { left: 'Fixed dividend paid first', right: 'Preferred stock' }, { left: 'Unlimited upside and usually a vote', right: 'Common stock' }, { left: 'Skipped dividends must be caught up', right: 'Cumulative preferred' }, { left: 'Paid before any shareholder in a bankruptcy', right: 'Creditors' } ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Share classes: some votes count more', html: `<p>Here is a twist. Some companies have several <span class="term" data-def="Different types of common stock from the same company, usually with different voting rights.">share classes</span>, and they do not all vote the same.</p><p>The classic example is Alphabet, Google's parent. <strong>Class A</strong> shares (ticker GOOGL) get one vote each. <strong>Class C</strong> shares (GOOG) get <em>no</em> vote. <strong>Class B</strong> shares carry ten votes each, are not publicly traded, and are held mostly by the founders and insiders. That setup lets the founders keep control even though they own a minority of the economic value. Meta uses a similar high-vote Class B structure.</p><p>Berkshire Hathaway shows a different reason for classes. Its Class A shares cost hundreds of thousands of dollars each, so in 1996 it created cheaper <strong>Class B</strong> shares, each worth a small fraction of an A share, with proportionally fewer votes.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'think', say: "Dual-class shares mean founders can outvote you forever. Sometimes that protects a long-term vision. Sometimes it protects a bad CEO. Check before you buy." } },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'A share of Alphabet Class C (GOOG) gives you the same voting power as a share of Class A (GOOGL).', answer: false, explain: 'Class A shares get one vote each. Class C shares have no vote. Their claim on profits is the same, which is why their prices usually trade close together.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Counting the slices', html: `<p>Three numbers describe how many slices exist:</p><ul><li><strong>Authorized shares:</strong> the maximum the company's charter allows it to issue. A ceiling, not a count.</li><li><strong>Shares outstanding:</strong> shares actually issued and held by investors, including insiders. This is the number that matters for your ownership percentage.</li><li><strong>Float:</strong> shares available for the public to trade, which excludes big locked-up or insider blocks.</li></ul><p>Why care? Your ownership is your shares divided by shares outstanding. If Ortiz Bakeries issues 1,000 new shares to fund a third location, Maya still has 10 shares, but now owns <span class="down">0.5%</span> instead of 1%. Whether that hurts depends on what the company does with the new money. More on that later in this chapter.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Lumen Bikes has 4,000,000 shares outstanding. Leo owns 2,000 shares. What percentage of the company does he own?', answer: 0.05, tolerance: 0.001, unit: '%', explain: '2,000 / 4,000,000 = 0.0005, which is 0.05%. Small, but he is still a part-owner.' },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Words to know', cards: [ { front: 'Share', back: 'One unit of ownership in a corporation.' }, { front: 'Dividend', back: 'Profits paid out in cash to shareholders, decided by the board.' }, { front: 'Residual claim', back: 'Shareholders get what is left after all creditors are paid.' }, { front: 'Preferred stock', back: 'A hybrid share with a fixed dividend and priority over common, but limited upside and usually no vote.' }, { front: 'Dual-class shares', back: 'Share classes with unequal votes, often used so founders keep control.' }, { front: 'Shares outstanding', back: 'All shares currently issued and held by investors.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'Maya owns 10 of the 1,000 shares of Ortiz Bakeries Inc. What does she own?', options: ['10 croissants a day', '1% of the business, including its profits and debts', 'A loan to the bakery that pays interest', 'The right to manage the bakery'], answer: 1, explain: 'A share is fractional ownership of the whole business. A loan would make her a creditor, and day-to-day management belongs to the CEO the board hires.' },
          { q: 'Which group is usually paid LAST if a company is liquidated?', options: ['Bondholders', 'Preferred shareholders', 'Common shareholders', 'Suppliers'], answer: 2, explain: 'Common shareholders are residual claimants. They get only what remains after creditors and preferred holders.' },
          { q: 'Leo buys 5 shares of a large company in his app. Where does his money go?', options: ['To the company, to fund new projects', 'To the stock exchange as a fee', 'Into a government insurance fund', 'Mostly to the investor who sold him the shares'], answer: 3, explain: 'On the secondary market, shares trade between investors. The company only raises cash when it issues new shares.' },
          { q: 'What is a key trade-off of preferred stock compared with common stock?', options: ['Priority and a steadier dividend in exchange for limited upside and usually no vote', 'More votes in exchange for no dividends', 'Higher risk in exchange for unlimited upside', 'Government backing in exchange for lower returns'], answer: 0, explain: 'Preferred holders get paid first and usually get a fixed dividend, but they rarely share in big growth and typically cannot vote.' },
          { q: 'Why might founders set up a company with dual-class shares?', options: ['To lower the share price for small investors', 'To keep voting control while selling most of the economic ownership', 'Because the SEC requires it for tech companies', 'To avoid paying dividends'], answer: 1, explain: 'High-vote shares let founders control the board even with a minority of the economic value. It is a choice, not a legal requirement.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
    {
      id: 'stocks-1-going-public',
      title: 'From Garage to Ticker Symbol',
      summary: 'How a startup goes from private funding to a public stock: venture capital, the IPO machine, direct listings, SPACs and lockups.',
      minutes: 14,
      icon: 'rocket',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Lumen Bikes is born', html: `<p>Two friends start <strong>Lumen Bikes</strong> in a garage, building e-bikes with lights built into the frame. At first it is their savings and a loan from a parent. Then they need real money.</p><p>Step by step, they sell slices of the company to private investors:</p><ul><li><span class="term" data-def="A wealthy individual who invests their own money in very early-stage startups.">Angel investors</span> put in the first outside cash.</li><li><span class="term" data-def="Funds that invest other people's money in young private companies in exchange for equity, hoping a few become huge.">Venture capital</span> firms lead bigger rounds, often called Seed, Series A, Series B and so on.</li></ul><p>Each round sells new shares at a price that implies a <span class="term" data-def="The total value investors put on a company, based on the price of its shares.">valuation</span>. If the business is growing, each round is priced higher. The founders own a smaller percentage after every round, but of something worth more.</p><p>All of this is <strong>private</strong>. You cannot buy Lumen on your brokerage app. Not yet.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: "Every giant public company was once a tiny private one with a scary bank balance and a big idea." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Why go public at all?', html: `<p>Eventually Lumen's board thinks about listing on a stock exchange. Reasons to do it:</p><ul><li><strong>Raise a lot of cash</strong> from many investors at once.</li><li><strong>Liquidity.</strong> Early investors and employees with stock can finally sell.</li><li><strong>A currency.</strong> Public shares can pay for acquisitions or attract employees.</li><li><strong>Visibility.</strong> Being public can boost the brand.</li></ul><p>Reasons to hesitate: it is expensive, every quarter's results become public, and short-term stock moves get a lot of attention. Public companies must file detailed reports with the <span class="term" data-def="The Securities and Exchange Commission, the US regulator for securities markets and public companies.">SEC</span> and live with outside shareholders second-guessing them. Some companies decide to stay private for a long time because private funding is plentiful.</p>` },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'ipo-path', caption: 'Follow a company from founders and venture rounds to the IPO and public trading. Notice that the company raises cash at the IPO, then shares change hands between investors.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The IPO machine', html: `<p>The classic route is an <span class="term" data-def="Initial public offering: the first time a company sells shares to the public and lists on an exchange.">IPO</span>. Here is the usual sequence:</p><ol><li><strong>Hire underwriters.</strong> Investment banks that organize the deal and line up buyers.</li><li><strong>File an S-1.</strong> A registration statement with the SEC laying out the business, financials and risks. Anyone can read it.</li><li><strong>The roadshow.</strong> Management pitches big investors, like mutual funds and pension funds, over days of meetings.</li><li><strong>Bookbuilding.</strong> Bankers collect orders: who wants how many shares, at what price.</li><li><strong>Pricing.</strong> Usually the evening before trading, the company and bankers set the final IPO price.</li><li><strong>First trade.</strong> The stock opens on the exchange and anyone can buy.</li></ol><p>The banks are paid with a <span class="term" data-def="The underwriters' fee: the difference between the IPO price and what the company actually receives per share.">gross spread</span>, often several percent of the money raised.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Lumen Bikes sells 10 million new shares in its IPO at $20 each. The underwriters keep a 7% gross spread. How much does Lumen receive, in millions of dollars?', answer: 186, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '$', explain: '10 million x $20 = $200 million raised. The banks keep 7%, which is $14 million, so Lumen gets $186 million (before other costs like lawyers and accountants).' },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'fact', title: 'The greenshoe', html: `<p>Most US IPOs include an <span class="term" data-def="An option letting underwriters sell up to 15% more shares than planned, used to help steady the price after the IPO.">over-allotment option</span>, nicknamed the greenshoe after the first company to use one. It lets underwriters sell up to <strong>15% more shares</strong> than planned. If the stock sags below the IPO price in the first weeks, the banks can buy shares back in the market, which helps support the price. If it rises, they exercise the option and the company sells the extra shares.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The first-day pop', html: `<p>Lumen prices at $20. The next morning the first trade prints at <span class="up">$29</span>. Headlines cheer a "45% pop."</p><p>Here is the uncomfortable part. Lumen sold its shares at <strong>$20</strong>. The extra $9 per share went to whoever got shares at the IPO price, mostly big institutional clients of the banks. Economists call this <span class="term" data-def="Setting the IPO price below what the market will pay, so the stock tends to jump on day one.">underpricing</span>, and a big pop means the company arguably left money on the table.</p><p>Why do bankers price below demand? A pop makes the deal look like a success and rewards the investors who took the risk. Regular investors usually cannot get the IPO price at all. Their first chance is often the opening trade, after the pop. Plenty of hot IPOs have later traded below where they opened, and some below the IPO price.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Buying a hot IPO at the open means paying the post-pop price, with little trading history and lots of hype. That is a lot of unknowns at once." } },
        { type: 'check', question: "Lumen's IPO is priced at $20 and opens at $29. Who captures that $9 jump on the shares sold in the IPO?", options: ['Lumen Bikes, the company', 'The investors who were allocated shares at $20', 'The stock exchange', 'Retail investors who bought at the open'], answer: 1, explain: 'The company sold at $20 and gets $20 (minus fees). The investors allocated shares at the IPO price own the gain. People buying at the open paid $29.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Direct listings: skip the middle', html: `<p>Some companies skip the traditional IPO. In a <span class="term" data-def="Listing existing shares directly on an exchange without a traditional underwritten offering. The opening price is set by buy and sell orders.">direct listing</span>, existing shareholders simply start selling on the exchange. There is no roadshow-set price. Instead, the exchange runs an opening auction that matches buy and sell orders to find the first price.</p><p>Well-known examples include Spotify (2018), Slack (2019) and Coinbase (2021). The appeal: no big underwriting spread, no day-one pop going to bank clients, and often no lockup forcing insiders to wait.</p><p>The catch: a classic direct listing does not raise new money for the company, since it just lets existing shares trade. Exchange rules have since been changed to allow raising money in a direct listing, but it remains far less common than the traditional IPO.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'SPACs: the blank-check shortcut', html: `<p>A <span class="term" data-def="Special purpose acquisition company: a shell company that IPOs with no business, then uses the cash to merge with a private company.">SPAC</span> is a company with no business at all. It raises cash in its own IPO, traditionally at about $10 a unit, parks the money in a trust, and goes hunting for a private company to merge with, usually within about two years. When it finds one, the merger (the "de-SPAC") turns that private company into a public one.</p><p>SPAC shareholders can usually vote on the deal and redeem their shares for their slice of the trust if they do not like it. If no deal happens, the money goes back.</p><p>SPACs boomed in 2020 and 2021, and many companies that went public this way later traded far below $10. Critics pointed to rosy projections and sponsor shares that diluted everyone else. The SEC adopted tougher SPAC disclosure rules in 2024.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "SPAC sponsors typically get a big chunk of shares for a small price. Read who gets what before assuming you are on the same team." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The lockup cliff', html: `<p>After a traditional IPO, insiders and early investors usually sign a <span class="term" data-def="An agreement that stops insiders and pre-IPO investors from selling shares for a set period after the IPO.">lockup</span> promising not to sell for a while, commonly around <strong>180 days</strong>, though terms vary and some deals release shares in stages.</p><p>Why? If every venture fund dumped its shares on day two, the price could collapse. The lockup keeps supply off the market while the stock finds its footing.</p><p>When the lockup ends, a lot of shares can suddenly become sellable. Traders watch lockup expiration dates on the calendar because the extra supply can weigh on the price. It does not always happen, but it is a known pressure point. The dates are disclosed in the IPO paperwork, so nobody should be surprised.</p>` },
        { type: 'order', prompt: 'Put the steps of a traditional IPO in order.', items: ['Hire underwriters', 'File an S-1 registration statement with the SEC', 'Roadshow and bookbuilding with big investors', 'Set the final IPO price', 'Shares begin trading on the exchange', 'Lockup period expires'], explain: 'Banks come first, then the public filing, then the marketing and order-taking, then pricing the night before trading. The lockup typically ends months later.' },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each way of going public, or IPO term, to its description.', pairs: [ { left: 'Traditional IPO', right: 'Underwriters sell new shares at a negotiated price' }, { left: 'Direct listing', right: 'Existing shares start trading, price found by auction' }, { left: 'SPAC', right: 'A cash shell that merges with a private company' }, { left: 'Lockup', right: 'Insiders wait months before selling' } ] },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'A bigger first-day pop always means the IPO was better for the company that sold its shares.', answer: false, explain: 'The company sold at the IPO price. A huge pop means buyers valued the shares far higher, so the company could have raised more money for the same slice.' },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'What is the main job of underwriters in an IPO?', options: ['Approving the company for listing on behalf of the SEC', 'Organizing the offering, gauging demand and selling shares to investors', 'Guaranteeing the stock will rise', 'Auditing the company accounts'], answer: 1, explain: 'Investment banks run the deal: due diligence, roadshow, bookbuilding and pricing. Auditors check the accounts, and nobody guarantees returns.' },
          { q: 'What document does a company file with the SEC before a US IPO?', options: ['A 10-K', 'An 8-K', 'An S-1 registration statement', 'A proxy statement'], answer: 2, explain: 'The S-1 lays out the business, financials and risks for prospective investors. The 10-K and 8-K come later, once the company is public.' },
          { q: 'Which statement about a classic direct listing is true?', options: ['It does not raise new money for the company', 'It always includes a 180-day lockup', 'Underwriters set the opening price the night before', 'It requires merging with a shell company'], answer: 0, explain: 'In a classic direct listing, existing shares simply begin trading. The price comes from an opening auction. Merging with a shell describes a SPAC.' },
          { q: 'Why might a stock drop around its lockup expiration?', options: ['The company must buy back shares', 'The SEC halts trading', 'Dividends are cancelled', 'Many insider shares suddenly become available to sell'], answer: 3, explain: 'Lockups keep insider supply off the market. When they end, a wave of potential selling can pressure the price.' },
          { q: 'In a SPAC, what can shareholders usually do if they dislike the proposed merger?', options: ['Nothing, the sponsor decides alone', 'Redeem their shares for their portion of the trust', 'Convert their shares into bonds', 'Force an IPO instead'], answer: 1, explain: 'SPAC investors typically have redemption rights, letting them get back their share of the cash held in trust.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
    {
      id: 'stocks-1-market-cap',
      title: 'Big, Small, and the $20 Illusion',
      summary: 'Market cap is the price tag on the whole company. Learn how to calculate it, what mega-cap and small-cap mean, and why a $20 stock is not cheaper than a $500 one.',
      minutes: 12,
      icon: 'scale',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'The price tag on the whole company', html: `<p>A stock price tells you what <em>one slice</em> costs. To know what the whole company is worth to the market, multiply by the number of slices:</p><p><span class="hl">Market cap = shares outstanding x share price</span></p><p><span class="term" data-def="Market capitalization: the total market value of a company's shares, equal to shares outstanding times the share price.">Market cap</span> is the number analysts actually use when they say a company is "worth" a certain amount. It moves every second the market is open, because the price does.</p><p>Say Lumen Bikes, now public, has <strong>50 million shares</strong> trading at <strong>$40</strong>. Its market cap is 50 million x $40 = <strong>$2 billion</strong>. If the stock rises to $44, the market cap becomes $2.2 billion. Nobody built a single extra bike. The market simply decided the business is worth more.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'happy', say: "Market cap is just multiplication. Price times shares. Owls love easy math that sounds fancy." } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Pebble Coffee Co. has 300 million shares outstanding and trades at $25. What is its market cap, in billions of dollars?', answer: 7.5, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: '300 million x $25 = $7.5 billion.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The $20 illusion', html: `<p>Dev scrolls his app and sees two stocks:</p><ul><li><strong>Pebble Coffee:</strong> $25 a share</li><li><strong>Northwind Software:</strong> $500 a share</li></ul><p>"Pebble is way cheaper," he says. Not so fast. Northwind has only 10 million shares, so its market cap is $5 billion. Pebble's is $7.5 billion. The "cheap" stock is the <em>bigger</em> company.</p><p>A share price on its own says nothing about whether a stock is cheap or expensive. Think of a pizza. Cut it into 8 slices or 80, it is the same pizza. A $500 slice of a small pizza can be a better deal than a $25 slice of a giant one. What matters is the price compared to what you get: profits, cash flow, assets. You will learn ratios for that in the next chapter.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'think', say: "Low share price does not mean bargain. It just means the company cut its pizza into more slices." } },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Stock A trades at $15 and Stock B at $300. What can you conclude about which is the better value?', options: ['A is cheaper because the price is lower', 'B is better because expensive stocks are higher quality', 'Nothing yet, you need to compare price with earnings, cash flow or assets', 'A has more room to grow because it is closer to zero'], answer: 2, explain: 'Share price alone tells you nothing about value. You need to know how many shares exist and what the business earns per share.' },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'myth', title: 'Myth: A $2 stock cannot fall much further', html: `<p>Dev once bought a stock at $2 because "how much lower can it go?" The answer: <span class="down">-100%</span>, same as any other stock. A $2 stock falling to $1 loses half its value, exactly like a $200 stock falling to $100. Percentages are what matter. And very low-priced stocks are often low because the business is struggling, not because they are on sale. Dev's $2 stock ended at 40 cents, an <span class="down">-80%</span> lesson.</p>` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', title: 'Fractional shares', html: `<p>Many brokers now let you buy <span class="term" data-def="A piece of one share, letting you invest a dollar amount instead of buying whole shares.">fractional shares</span>, so a $500 stock does not require $500. Maya could put $20 into Northwind and own 0.04 of a share. One more reason the share price itself is not the point. Check whether your broker offers fractions and for which stocks.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Mega, large, mid, small, micro', html: `<p>Investors sort companies into size buckets. These cutoffs are <strong>conventions, not laws</strong>. Different index providers and fund companies draw the lines differently, and they drift upward as markets grow. One common rough version:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Bucket</th><th>Rough market cap</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mega-cap</td><td>Above about $200 billion</td></tr><tr><td>Large-cap</td><td>About $10 billion to $200 billion</td></tr><tr><td>Mid-cap</td><td>About $2 billion to $10 billion</td></tr><tr><td>Small-cap</td><td>About $300 million to $2 billion</td></tr><tr><td>Micro-cap</td><td>Below about $300 million</td></tr></tbody></table><p>So Lumen at $2 billion sits right on the small/mid border, and Pebble at $7.5 billion is a mid-cap. The very largest companies today are worth trillions, which makes even "mega" feel like an understatement.</p>` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Using the rough conventions above, match each company to its size bucket.', pairs: [ { left: '$450 billion market cap', right: 'Mega-cap' }, { left: '$40 billion market cap', right: 'Large-cap' }, { left: '$5 billion market cap', right: 'Mid-cap' }, { left: '$800 million market cap', right: 'Small-cap' }, { left: '$90 million market cap', right: 'Micro-cap' } ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Why size changes the ride', html: `<p>Size is not just a label. It tends to change how a stock behaves.</p><p><strong>Large companies</strong> usually have many business lines, steady access to borrowing, lots of analysts studying them, and huge daily trading volume, so you can buy or sell easily with a tight <span class="term" data-def="The gap between the highest price buyers are bidding and the lowest price sellers are asking.">bid-ask spread</span>.</p><p><strong>Smaller companies</strong> have more room to grow (going from $500 million to $5 billion is easier than going from $500 billion to $5 trillion), but they are often more volatile, more fragile in recessions, and less studied. Some research has found small stocks earned higher long-run returns, but that effect has been inconsistent and is still debated.</p><p>Micro-caps are a special zone: thin trading, wide spreads, sometimes sparse disclosures. That is where many pump-and-dump schemes live.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "If you see a tiny stock hyped in a group chat with rocket talk, ask who is selling to you. In micro-caps it is often the hypers." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Market cap is not the whole price', html: `<p>Market cap measures the value of the company's <strong>shares</strong>. But if you wanted to buy an entire business, you would also take on its debt and get its cash.</p><p>Think of buying a house listed at $300,000 that comes with a $100,000 unpaid loan you must take over, plus $20,000 cash hidden in the attic. The real cost to you is about $380,000, not $300,000.</p><p>That is why analysts also use <span class="term" data-def="Enterprise value: roughly market cap plus debt minus cash. The price to buy the whole business, debt included.">enterprise value (EV)</span>:</p><p><span class="hl">EV is roughly market cap + debt - cash</span></p><p>Two companies with the same $10 billion market cap can be very different deals if one owes $8 billion and the other has $3 billion in the bank. You will use EV again in the valuation lesson of the next chapter.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Pebble Coffee has a $7.5 billion market cap, $2 billion of debt and $0.5 billion of cash. What is its approximate enterprise value, in billions of dollars?', answer: 9, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: 'EV is about market cap + debt - cash: 7.5 + 2 - 0.5 = $9 billion.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Why the giants move the index', html: `<p>Market cap also decides how much a company counts in many stock indexes. The S&P 500 is <span class="term" data-def="An index where bigger companies get a bigger weight, based on their market value.">market-cap weighted</span> (adjusted for float), so a company worth $3 trillion moves the index far more than one worth $30 billion.</p><p>That is why you sometimes see the S&P 500 rise on a day when most of its stocks fell: a few giants had a great day. In recent years the largest handful of tech-heavy companies have made up an unusually big share of the index, which means an "index fund" can be more concentrated than people assume. Check a fund's top holdings to see what you really own.</p><p>Many other indexes, and most index funds that track them, are weighted the same way. So as companies grow, they automatically take up more room in your fund, without anyone deciding to buy more.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'wow', say: "An equal-weight index gives every company the same slice instead. Same 500 stocks, very different ride." } },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'If a company has more shares outstanding than another, it must have a bigger market cap.', answer: false, explain: 'Market cap is shares times price. A company with fewer shares at a much higher price can easily be worth more.' },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Words to know', cards: [ { front: 'Market cap', back: 'Shares outstanding times share price. What the market values the equity at.' }, { front: 'Enterprise value', back: 'Roughly market cap plus debt minus cash. The cost of the whole business.' }, { front: 'Mid-cap', back: 'Roughly $2 billion to $10 billion in market cap, by one common convention.' }, { front: 'Cap-weighted index', back: 'An index where bigger companies count for more.' }, { front: 'Fractional share', back: 'Part of a share, bought with a dollar amount.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'Northwind Software has 10 million shares at $500. Pebble Coffee has 300 million shares at $25. Which is bigger by market cap?', options: ['Northwind, because its share price is higher', 'Pebble, at $7.5 billion vs $5 billion', 'They are the same size', 'You cannot compare companies with different share prices'], answer: 1, explain: 'Northwind: 10M x $500 = $5B. Pebble: 300M x $25 = $7.5B. Share price alone is misleading.' },
          { q: 'Which statement about market-cap size buckets is most accurate?', options: ['They are set by the SEC', 'They never change', 'They are rough conventions that vary by source and drift over time', 'They depend on share price only'], answer: 2, explain: 'Mega, large, mid, small and micro are conventions. Different index providers use different cutoffs.' },
          { q: 'Leo thinks a $12 stock is a bargain because it is "cheap." What is the best response?', options: ['He is right, low prices mean more upside', 'Price per share means nothing without comparing it to what each share earns or owns', 'Stocks under $20 are always riskier', 'He should only buy stocks above $100'], answer: 1, explain: 'Value comes from comparing price to earnings, cash flow or assets per share, not the price tag alone.' },
          { q: 'Two companies each have a $10B market cap. Company X has $6B of debt and $1B cash. Company Y has no debt and $2B cash. Which has the higher enterprise value?', options: ['Company Y', 'They are equal', 'It depends on the share price', 'Company X'], answer: 3, explain: 'X: 10 + 6 - 1 = $15B. Y: 10 + 0 - 2 = $8B. Buying all of X means taking on a lot of debt.' },
          { q: 'Why can the S&P 500 rise on a day when most of its stocks fall?', options: ['Because it is market-cap weighted, so a few giants can outweigh many smaller losers', 'Because it only counts tech stocks', 'Because falling stocks are removed that day', 'Because it is equal weighted'], answer: 0, explain: 'In a cap-weighted index, the largest companies have the biggest influence on the daily move.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
    {
      id: 'stocks-1-sectors',
      title: 'The 11 Neighborhoods of the Market',
      summary: 'Every stock lives in a sector. Learn the 11 GICS sectors, which ones swing with the economy, which ones hold steady, and how interest rates hit each differently.',
      minutes: 13,
      icon: 'globe',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Why sort companies at all?', html: `<p>There are thousands of public companies. To compare them sensibly, investors group similar businesses together. The most widely used system is <span class="term" data-def="Global Industry Classification Standard: a system created by S&P and MSCI that sorts companies into sectors, industry groups, industries and sub-industries.">GICS</span>, created in 1999 by S&P and MSCI.</p><p>GICS works like a postal address with four levels, from broad to narrow:</p><ol><li><strong>Sector</strong> (11 of them)</li><li><strong>Industry group</strong></li><li><strong>Industry</strong></li><li><strong>Sub-industry</strong></li></ol><p>A company is placed based mainly on where its revenue comes from. Lumen Bikes, for instance, would land in Consumer Discretionary, the sector for things people <em>want</em> but can skip in a pinch.</p><p>Why it matters: companies in the same sector often react to the same forces, like oil prices, interest rates or consumer spending. And comparing a bank with a software company is like comparing a hamster with a horse. Sectors help you compare like with like.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'happy', say: "Sectors are neighborhoods. Companies on the same street tend to get hit by the same weather." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Meet the 11 sectors', html: `<table><thead><tr><th>Sector</th><th>Typical businesses</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Information Technology</td><td>Software, chips, computers, IT services</td></tr><tr><td>Communication Services</td><td>Telecom, media, entertainment, internet platforms, video games</td></tr><tr><td>Consumer Discretionary</td><td>Cars, restaurants, hotels, apparel, online retail</td></tr><tr><td>Consumer Staples</td><td>Food, drinks, household products, grocery stores</td></tr><tr><td>Health Care</td><td>Drugmakers, biotech, medical devices, health insurers</td></tr><tr><td>Financials</td><td>Banks, insurers, asset managers, payment networks</td></tr><tr><td>Industrials</td><td>Aerospace, machinery, airlines, railroads</td></tr><tr><td>Energy</td><td>Oil and gas producers, refiners, pipelines</td></tr><tr><td>Materials</td><td>Chemicals, mining, steel, packaging</td></tr><tr><td>Utilities</td><td>Electric, gas and water providers</td></tr><tr><td>Real Estate</td><td>REITs, property owners and developers</td></tr></tbody></table>` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each business to its GICS sector.', pairs: [ { left: 'A toothpaste and detergent maker', right: 'Consumer Staples' }, { left: 'A semiconductor designer', right: 'Information Technology' }, { left: 'An oil and gas driller', right: 'Energy' }, { left: 'A regional electric company', right: 'Utilities' }, { left: 'A hotel chain', right: 'Consumer Discretionary' } ] },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'fact', title: 'The labels change', html: `<p>GICS is updated over time. Real Estate was split out of Financials as its own sector in 2016. In 2018 the old Telecommunication Services sector became <strong>Communication Services</strong> and picked up media and internet companies. In 2023, payment networks such as Visa and Mastercard moved from Information Technology to Financials. Sector labels are human choices, so always check what a sector fund actually holds.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Cyclical vs defensive', html: `<p>Imagine a recession hits and Leo's company freezes raises. What does he cut?</p><p>He postpones the new car, skips the weekend trip, and eats out less. Those businesses are <span class="term" data-def="Companies whose sales and profits rise and fall strongly with the economy.">cyclical</span>: their sales swing with the economy. Consumer Discretionary, Industrials, Materials, Energy and Financials tend to be the most cyclical sectors.</p><p>But Leo still buys toothpaste, groceries and medicine, and still pays the electric bill. Those businesses are <span class="term" data-def="Companies whose sales stay relatively steady in good times and bad because people keep buying their products.">defensive</span>. Consumer Staples, Utilities and Health Care are the classic defensive sectors.</p><p>The trade-off: cyclicals often rebound hard when the economy recovers, while defensives tend to lag in booms but hold up better in downturns. Neither is better. They just behave differently.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'think', say: "Recession test: would you stop buying it if money got tight? If yes, it is probably cyclical." } },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Which business is most likely to keep steady sales during a recession?', options: ['A luxury car maker', 'A cruise line', 'A maker of household cleaning products', 'A steel producer'], answer: 2, explain: 'Household staples are bought in good times and bad, so their sales are defensive. Luxury cars, cruises and steel are classic cyclicals.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'How interest rates hit each sector', html: `<p>When interest rates rise, sectors feel it differently. These are tendencies, not rules:</p><ul><li><strong>Utilities and Real Estate</strong> often carry lots of debt and are owned for their steady dividends. Higher rates raise their borrowing costs and make safe bonds a tougher competitor for income investors.</li><li><strong>Fast-growing tech</strong> companies earn much of their expected profit far in the future. Higher rates shrink what those future dollars are worth today, a math idea you will meet in the value investing chapter.</li><li><strong>Homebuilders and car sellers</strong> suffer when mortgages and auto loans get pricier.</li><li><strong>Banks</strong> can earn a wider gap between what they charge borrowers and pay depositors, but sharp rate jumps can also cause losses on bonds they hold and more loan defaults.</li></ul>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Rates, inflation and growth all move at once in real life. Do not bet the farm on a textbook sector reaction." } },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'Rising interest rates always hurt bank stocks.', answer: false, explain: 'Higher rates can widen the gap between what banks earn on loans and pay on deposits. They can also cause bond losses and defaults. The effect depends on how and why rates rise.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The economy and sector rotation', html: `<p>Some investors try <span class="term" data-def="Shifting money between sectors based on where the economy seems to be in the business cycle.">sector rotation</span>: moving money toward sectors expected to do well in each phase of the business cycle. The textbook story goes like this:</p><ul><li><strong>Early recovery:</strong> cyclicals like Consumer Discretionary, Financials and Industrials often lead.</li><li><strong>Late expansion:</strong> Energy and Materials can benefit from strong demand and rising prices.</li><li><strong>Recession:</strong> defensives like Staples, Utilities and Health Care tend to fall less.</li></ul><p>In practice, it is messy. Nobody announces the start of a recession in real time, and markets often move months before the economy does. Stocks tend to start recovering while the economic news still looks awful.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Every sector has its own normal', html: `<p>Sectors also have different "normal" numbers. Software companies can have very high profit margins, while grocery stores often keep only a few cents of each dollar of sales. Utilities usually pay large dividends and grow slowly. Biotech companies may have no profits at all for years while testing drugs.</p><p>Energy and Materials companies ride commodity prices. When oil jumps, oil producers' profits can soar, then shrink when prices fall. That makes a single year's earnings a shaky guide.</p><p>So when you compare a stock's numbers, compare it to <strong>its own sector</strong> first. A margin that is great for a grocer could be alarming for a software maker. You will put this into practice with valuation ratios in the next chapter.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "A thin margin is normal for a supermarket. The same margin at a software company would make me fall off my branch." } },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', title: 'Is a bakery a staple?', html: `<p>Here is where it gets fuzzy. If Ortiz Bakeries sold packaged bread in supermarkets, it would likely be classed under <strong>Consumer Staples</strong> (packaged foods). If it ran sit-down cafés selling fancy pastries, it would land in <strong>Consumer Discretionary</strong> (restaurants). Same flour, different business model. During a downturn, the supermarket bread keeps selling while the café visits get cut first. The label follows how the money is made, and so does the risk.</p>` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'warn', title: 'Sector funds are concentrated bets', html: `<p>Sector funds let you buy a whole neighborhood at once. Handy, but a single-sector fund is far less diversified than a broad index fund, and some sector funds are dominated by just a few giant companies. Look at the top holdings before assuming you own a broad slice of anything.</p>` },
        { type: 'order', prompt: 'Put the levels of GICS in order from broadest to narrowest.', items: ['Sector', 'Industry group', 'Industry', 'Sub-industry'], explain: 'GICS has four levels. There are 11 sectors at the top and many sub-industries at the bottom.' },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Words to know', cards: [ { front: 'GICS', back: 'The standard system sorting companies into 11 sectors and finer groups.' }, { front: 'Cyclical', back: 'Sales and profits swing with the economy.' }, { front: 'Defensive', back: 'Sales stay steadier in recessions because people keep buying.' }, { front: 'Sector rotation', back: 'Shifting money between sectors based on the business cycle.' }, { front: 'Rate sensitive', back: 'Strongly affected by changes in interest rates, like utilities and real estate.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'How many sectors does GICS currently have?', options: ['9', '10', '12', '11'], answer: 3, explain: 'GICS has 11 sectors, after Real Estate was added in 2016.' },
          { q: 'Which group lists three classic defensive sectors?', options: ['Energy, Materials, Industrials', 'Consumer Staples, Utilities, Health Care', 'Technology, Financials, Real Estate', 'Consumer Discretionary, Energy, Utilities'], answer: 1, explain: 'People keep buying food, power and medicine in recessions, so these sectors are the classic defensives.' },
          { q: 'Why do utility stocks often struggle when interest rates rise?', options: ['They carry heavy debt and compete with bonds for income-seeking investors', 'They stop paying dividends by law', 'Electricity demand drops to zero', 'They are not allowed to borrow'], answer: 0, explain: 'Higher rates raise their borrowing costs and make bond yields more attractive compared with utility dividends.' },
          { q: 'In 2023, where did GICS move payment networks like Visa and Mastercard?', options: ['From Financials to Information Technology', 'From Industrials to Financials', 'From Information Technology to Financials', 'From Communication Services to Financials'], answer: 2, explain: 'They moved from Information Technology to Financials. Sector labels change over time.' },
          { q: 'Why is comparing a grocery chain profit margin with a software company margin misleading?', options: ['Grocery margins are secret', 'Different sectors have very different normal margins', 'Software companies do not report margins', 'Margins only matter for banks'], answer: 1, explain: 'Each sector has its own normal ranges. Compare companies with sector peers first.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
    {
      id: 'stocks-1-splits-buybacks-dilution',
      title: 'Splits, Buybacks and the Shrinking Slice',
      summary: 'Why a stock split changes nothing, why buybacks can boost earnings per share, and how new shares quietly dilute existing owners.',
      minutes: 14,
      icon: 'calculator',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Same pizza, more slices', html: `<p>Lumen Bikes stock has climbed to <strong>$600</strong>. The board announces a <span class="term" data-def="A company increases its number of shares and lowers the price per share proportionally. Total value is unchanged.">2-for-1 stock split</span>. The next morning, every shareholder has twice as many shares, each priced around <strong>$300</strong>.</p><p>Leo had 10 shares worth $6,000. Now he has 20 shares worth... $6,000. The company is worth exactly the same. Its profits, its debt, its bikes: unchanged. Only the slicing changed.</p><p>So why bother? Mostly for convenience and psychology. A lower share price can feel more approachable to small investors and employees, and it makes standard options contracts, which usually cover 100 shares, cheaper per contract. With fractional shares now common, the practical reason has faded, but companies still do it.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: "Cutting a pizza into more slices does not make more pizza. Splits are the same idea with a press release." } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'stock-split', caption: 'One share becomes several, and the price per share drops by the same factor. Your total value stays the same before and after.' },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Maya owns 15 shares of a stock trading at $600. The company does a 3-for-1 split. How many shares does she own afterward?', answer: 45, tolerance: 0, unit: '', explain: 'A 3-for-1 split triples the share count: 15 x 3 = 45 shares, each worth about $200. Her total stays about $9,000.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Famous splits', html: `<p>Some well-known examples:</p><ul><li><strong>Apple</strong> split 4-for-1 in 2020.</li><li><strong>Tesla</strong> split 5-for-1 in 2020 and 3-for-1 in 2022.</li><li><strong>Nvidia</strong> split 4-for-1 in 2021 and 10-for-1 in 2024.</li></ul><p>Stocks sometimes rise after a split is announced. That is probably not the split creating value. Companies usually split after their stock has run up a lot, often because business is going well, and a split can signal management's confidence. The split itself is just accounting.</p><p>One thing to watch: when you look at a long-term price chart, make sure it is <span class="term" data-def="Historical prices recalculated to account for splits (and sometimes dividends) so the chart is comparable over time.">split-adjusted</span>. Otherwise it will look like the stock crashed 90% on split day.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "Data sites adjust old prices for splits. If a chart shows a sudden cliff with no news, check for a split before you panic." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Reverse splits: a warning light', html: `<p>A <span class="term" data-def="Combining several shares into one, raising the price per share proportionally. Total value is unchanged at that moment.">reverse split</span> runs the other way. In a 1-for-10 reverse split, every 10 shares become 1 and the price rises 10x. A $0.80 stock becomes an $8 stock. Value is unchanged at that moment.</p><p>Healthy, growing companies rarely need this. Reverse splits are often done by struggling companies whose stock has fallen so far that it risks breaking an exchange rule. Both Nasdaq and the NYSE generally require listed stocks to keep a price of at least <strong>$1</strong>, and a company that stays below that for too long can be delisted.</p><p>A reverse split fixes the price tag, not the business. If the problems that sank the stock are still there, the price can keep sliding after the split.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "A reverse split is often a company putting makeup on a falling stock. Ask why the price got that low in the first place." } },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', title: "Dev's split FOMO", html: `<p>Dev hears a popular stock will split 10-for-1 next week and rushes to buy before the date. "Free shares!" he says. After the split he does have ten times as many shares, each worth one-tenth as much. Same money, more rows in his app. Any gain or loss he gets comes from the stock moving, not from the split. Splits are a non-event for value, even when they are a big event for headlines.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'A 1-for-10 reverse split instantly makes your investment worth 10 times more.', answer: false, explain: 'You end up with one-tenth as many shares at ten times the price. Your total value is unchanged at that moment.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Buybacks: shrinking the pie count', html: `<p>Now something that <em>does</em> change your slice. In a <span class="term" data-def="When a company uses its cash to buy its own shares, reducing the number of shares outstanding.">buyback</span>, the company uses cash to buy its own shares on the market, then usually retires them. Fewer shares outstanding means each remaining share owns a bigger piece of the business.</p><p>Watch what it does to <span class="term" data-def="Earnings per share: net income divided by shares outstanding.">EPS</span>, earnings per share:</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Before</th><th>After buyback</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Net income</td><td>$300M</td><td>$300M</td></tr><tr><td>Shares</td><td>300M</td><td>280M</td></tr><tr><td>EPS</td><td>$1.00</td><td><span class="up">about $1.07</span></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Profit did not grow at all, yet EPS rose about 7%, because the same profit is split among fewer shares. Owners who did not sell now own a slightly bigger slice.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Lumen Bikes earns $100 million with 50 million shares outstanding (EPS = $2.00). It buys back and retires 5 million shares. If net income stays at $100 million, what is the new EPS?', answer: 2.22, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: 'New share count is 45 million. $100M / 45M is about $2.22. EPS rose about 11% with zero profit growth.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Good buybacks, bad buybacks', html: `<p>Buybacks are not automatically good. A buyback is the company spending <em>your</em> cash to buy more of itself, so the price it pays matters.</p><ul><li><strong>Smart:</strong> buying shares when they trade below a sober estimate of the business's value, using spare cash the company does not need.</li><li><strong>Not so smart:</strong> buying at sky-high prices near a peak, borrowing heavily to fund buybacks, or cutting important investment to prop up EPS.</li></ul><p>Compared with dividends, buybacks are flexible. A company can pause them quietly, while cutting a dividend usually spooks investors. Buybacks can also be more tax-efficient for shareholders who do not sell, since they are not taxed on cash they never received.</p><p>One more trick to watch: when executive bonuses depend on EPS targets, buybacks can hit the target without the business improving at all.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Dilution: when your slice shrinks', html: `<p>Remember Ms. Ortiz issuing new shares? That is <span class="term" data-def="A reduction in existing owners' percentage of a company because new shares were issued.">dilution</span>. Companies add shares in several ways:</p><ul><li><strong>Secondary offerings:</strong> selling new shares to raise cash.</li><li><strong>Stock-based compensation:</strong> paying employees with shares, restricted stock units or options. A real cost, even though no cash leaves the building.</li><li><strong>Acquisitions paid in stock.</strong></li><li><strong>Convertible bonds</strong> that turn into shares later.</li></ul><p>Dilution is not always bad. If Ortiz Bakeries sells new shares at a fair price and uses the money to open a location that earns a great return, Maya owns a smaller slice of a bigger, more profitable pie. It hurts when shares are issued cheaply, or the money is wasted.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'warn', say: "Check the share count over five years. If it climbs a few percent every year, your slice is quietly shrinking." } },
        { type: 'check', question: 'A company spends $2 billion a year on buybacks, but its share count stays flat over five years. What is the most likely explanation?', options: ['The buybacks are mostly offsetting new shares issued to employees', 'Buybacks never reduce share count', 'The company is doing a reverse split each year', 'The stock price is not moving'], answer: 0, explain: 'Heavy stock-based compensation can add as many shares as buybacks remove. Then the buyback mostly cancels out dilution instead of shrinking the share count.' },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', title: 'Where to check the share count', html: `<p>Every annual report (the 10-K) lists shares outstanding on its cover page, and the income statement shows the weighted average share count used for EPS. Compare those numbers across several years. Falling share count: buybacks are shrinking the pie count. Rising share count: owners are being diluted. You will learn to read these reports in the next chapter.</p>` },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Words to know', cards: [ { front: 'Forward split', back: 'More shares at a proportionally lower price. Value unchanged.' }, { front: 'Reverse split', back: 'Fewer shares at a proportionally higher price. Often a sign of a struggling stock.' }, { front: 'Buyback', back: 'The company buys its own shares, so each remaining share owns more.' }, { front: 'EPS', back: 'Earnings per share: net income divided by shares outstanding.' }, { front: 'Dilution', back: 'Your ownership percentage shrinks because new shares were created.' }, { front: 'Stock-based compensation', back: 'Paying employees with shares or options. No cash out, but it dilutes owners.' } ] },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each action to what it does to your ownership.', pairs: [ { left: '2-for-1 forward split', right: 'More shares, same percentage ownership' }, { left: '1-for-10 reverse split', right: 'Fewer shares, same percentage ownership' }, { left: 'Buyback with retired shares', right: 'Your percentage ownership rises' }, { left: 'New shares issued to employees', right: 'Your percentage ownership falls' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'After a 4-for-1 split, a $400 stock will trade around...', options: ['$1,600', '$400', '$100', '$40'], answer: 2, explain: 'The price divides by 4 while share count multiplies by 4. Total value is unchanged.' },
          { q: 'Why do struggling companies often do reverse splits?', options: ['To keep the price above exchange minimums such as $1', 'To pay a special dividend', 'To raise new money from investors', 'Because the SEC requires one every year'], answer: 0, explain: 'Nasdaq and the NYSE generally require a minimum price of about $1. A reverse split lifts the price without changing the business.' },
          { q: 'Net income is flat, but a company retired 10% of its shares. What happens to EPS?', options: ['It falls 10%', 'It stays the same', 'It doubles', 'It rises about 11%'], answer: 3, explain: 'EPS = net income / shares. Dividing the same income by 90% as many shares raises EPS by 1 / 0.9 = about 11%.' },
          { q: 'Which buyback is most likely to create value for remaining shareholders?', options: ['Borrowing heavily to buy shares at an all-time-high valuation', 'Using spare cash to buy shares well below a sober estimate of their value', 'Cutting research spending to hit an EPS bonus target', 'Buying shares only to offset stock grants'], answer: 1, explain: 'A buyback is an investment in the company itself. Buying below value with spare cash helps the owners who stay.' },
          { q: 'Why is stock-based compensation a real cost to shareholders?', options: ['It is paid in cash', 'It is illegal', 'It creates new shares that dilute existing owners', 'It lowers the share price by law'], answer: 2, explain: "Paying employees in stock does not use cash, but it gives away slices of the company, shrinking every existing owner's percentage." },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
    {
      id: 'stocks-1-where-returns-come-from',
      title: 'Where Stock Returns Really Come From',
      summary: 'Total return is price change plus dividends. Underneath, it comes from three engines: earnings growth, changes in the valuation multiple, and cash paid out.',
      minutes: 14,
      icon: 'chart',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Two ways to get paid', html: `<p>Maya buys one share of Pebble Coffee for <strong>$50</strong>. A year later:</p><ul><li>The price is <strong>$54</strong>. That is a <span class="term" data-def="The gain from the share price going up.">price return</span> of $4.</li><li>Pebble paid four quarterly dividends of $0.375, so <strong>$1.50</strong> landed in her account.</li></ul><p>Her <span class="term" data-def="Price change plus dividends received, as a percentage of what you paid.">total return</span> is the price gain <em>plus</em> the dividends:</p><p><span class="hl">($4 + $1.50) / $50 = 11%</span></p><p>If she only looked at the price chart, she would think she made 8%. Dividends are easy to forget because they do not show up on the chart, but over many years they add up to a lot.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: "Price goes up, cash comes in. Total return counts both. Charts only show you half the party." } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Leo buys a stock at $80. A year later it trades at $76, and he received $2 in dividends. What was his total return, in percent?', answer: -2.5, tolerance: 0.05, unit: '%', explain: 'Price change is -$4, dividends +$2, so total is -$2. -2 / 80 = -2.5%. The dividend softened the loss, but did not erase it.' },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'dividend-flow', caption: 'Profits can flow to you as dividends. Reinvest them and they buy more shares, which pay more dividends next time.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Dividend yield and reinvesting', html: `<p>The <span class="term" data-def="Annual dividends per share divided by the share price.">dividend yield</span> tells you how much cash a stock pays relative to its price. Pebble pays $1.50 a year at a $50 price, a <strong>3% yield</strong>.</p><p>If Maya <span class="term" data-def="Automatically using dividends to buy more shares of the same stock or fund. Often called DRIP.">reinvests</span> those dividends, they buy more shares, which pay their own dividends next time. That is compounding again, the same snowball you met with savings accounts.</p><p>This is why serious long-term comparisons use <strong>total return</strong> data, which assumes dividends are reinvested. A price-only index chart understates what holders actually earned over decades.</p><p>Careful, though: a very high yield is not automatically good. If the price has crashed because investors expect a dividend cut, the yield looks juicy right before it shrinks.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "A suspiciously high yield is often the market whispering that the dividend is in danger. Ask why it is so high." } },
        { type: 'widget', name: 'dividend-income', props: { portfolio: 10000, yield: 3 }, caption: 'Change the portfolio size and yield to see how much annual cash dividends could produce. Notice how big a portfolio you need for meaningful income.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The three engines', html: `<p>Here is the key formula of the whole chapter. A share price can be written as:</p><p><span class="hl">Price = EPS x P/E multiple</span></p><p><span class="term" data-def="Price-to-earnings ratio: how many dollars investors pay for each dollar of annual earnings per share.">P/E</span> is how many dollars investors are willing to pay for each dollar of yearly earnings. So the price can rise for only two reasons: the company earns more (EPS up), or investors pay more per dollar of earnings (P/E up). Add the cash paid to you, and every stock's total return comes from three engines:</p><ol><li><strong>Earnings growth</strong></li><li><strong>Change in the multiple</strong> (investor mood and expectations)</li><li><strong>Dividends</strong> (plus buybacks, which boost EPS per share)</li></ol><p>Example: Pebble earns $2 per share at a P/E of 20, so the price is $40. Five years later EPS is $3. If the P/E holds at 20, the price is $60. If investors cool off and the P/E drops to 15, the price is only $45, despite 50% earnings growth.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Lumen Bikes earns $4 per share and trades at a P/E of 15. Five years later EPS has grown to $6, but the P/E has fallen to 12. What is the new share price?', answer: 72, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: 'Old price: $4 x 15 = $60. New price: $6 x 12 = $72. Earnings grew 50%, but the shrinking multiple ate much of it, so the price rose only 20%.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Mood in the short run, profits in the long run', html: `<p>Over a few months, most of a stock's move comes from the <strong>multiple</strong>, which is driven by news, fear, hype and interest rates. Earnings do not change that fast.</p><p>Over decades, it flips. Multiples swing up and down but cannot rise forever, because investors will not pay an infinite price for a dollar of earnings. Earnings, on the other hand, can compound for decades. So over long periods, <span class="hl">earnings growth and cash returned do most of the heavy lifting</span>.</p><p>Benjamin Graham, the father of value investing, put a version of this memorably: day to day the market behaves like a popularity contest, but over time it acts more like a scale that weighs what a business is really worth. You will meet Graham properly in the value investing chapter.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Pay a huge multiple and you need the business to grow fast just to break even if the mood cools. Expensive stocks have less room for disappointment." } },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'myth', title: 'Myth: Prices rise because more people buy than sell', html: `<p>Every trade has exactly one buyer and one seller, so there are never "more buyers than sellers" in shares traded. Prices rise when buyers are willing to pay <em>more</em> to get sellers to part with their shares, and fall when sellers accept <em>less</em>. What changes their willingness? Expectations about future profits, interest rates and plain old mood. That is the multiple engine at work.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: "Over ten years, a company's EPS doubled, its P/E went from 30 to 15, and it paid no dividends. Roughly what happened to the share price?", options: ['It doubled', 'It stayed about the same', 'It fell by half', 'It quadrupled'], answer: 1, explain: 'Price = EPS x P/E. Doubling EPS while halving the P/E leaves the price unchanged. Great business growth, zero price return, because the starting multiple was so high.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Why profits are the anchor', html: `<p>Think back to Ms. Ortiz. If you were buying the whole bakery from her, you would not care what the neighbors think it is worth this week. You would care how much cash it will hand you over the years. Stock investors are, on average and over time, doing the same calculation.</p><p>Why should profits drive prices at all? Because a share is a claim on the business's cash, now and in the future. Eventually that cash reaches owners in one of three ways: dividends, buybacks, or reinvestment that grows future profits. A business that never produces cash for its owners is, in the end, hard to justify paying for.</p><p>That is why companies with no profits are valued entirely on hopes about the future. Sometimes those hopes come true. Often they do not. In the dot-com bust, the tech-heavy Nasdaq Composite lost nearly 80% from its March 2000 peak to its 2002 low, and many profitless internet companies vanished entirely.</p><p>Price can drift far from profits for a long time. But profits, and the cash they produce, are the anchor that prices keep returning to.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'A company that never pays dividends cannot produce returns for its shareholders.', answer: false, explain: 'It can still raise EPS by growing profits or buying back shares, and the price can rise. Many successful companies paid no dividends for years while reinvesting.' },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'warn', title: 'Past returns are not a promise', html: `<p>Broad US stock indexes have historically produced strong long-run total returns, but with brutal drops along the way and long stretches of going nowhere. Starting valuations matter: buying when multiples are very high has historically been followed by weaker long-run returns than buying when they are low, although timing this precisely is not possible. Nothing guarantees the future will match the past.</p>` },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Words to know', cards: [ { front: 'Total return', back: 'Price change plus dividends, as a percentage of what you paid.' }, { front: 'Dividend yield', back: 'Annual dividend divided by share price.' }, { front: 'P/E multiple', back: 'Share price divided by earnings per share. What investors pay per dollar of earnings.' }, { front: 'Multiple expansion', back: 'Investors paying more per dollar of earnings, lifting the price without earnings growth.' }, { front: 'Reinvesting dividends', back: 'Using dividend cash to buy more shares so returns compound.' } ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'Maya buys at $40, sells a year later at $42, and collected $1.20 in dividends. Her total return is...', options: ['5%', '3%', '8%', '6%'], answer: 2, explain: '($2 price gain + $1.20 dividends) / $40 = $3.20 / $40 = 8%.' },
          { q: 'Which three engines drive a stock total return?', options: ['Earnings growth, change in the P/E multiple, and cash returned to shareholders', 'Revenue, share price and trading volume', 'Interest rates, inflation and taxes', 'Splits, reverse splits and lockups'], answer: 0, explain: 'Price = EPS x P/E, so price changes come from earnings and the multiple. Dividends and buybacks add the cash piece.' },
          { q: 'Over a few months, what usually explains most of a stock price movement?', options: ['Changes in dividends', 'Stock splits', 'Changes in shares outstanding', 'Changes in the multiple investors are willing to pay'], answer: 3, explain: 'Earnings change slowly. In the short run, shifts in mood, news and rates move the multiple, and that dominates.' },
          { q: 'A stock yields 12% while similar companies yield 3%. What is the most cautious interpretation?', options: ['It is a guaranteed bargain', 'The market may expect the dividend to be cut', 'Its earnings must be growing fastest', 'High yields mean low risk'], answer: 1, explain: 'An unusually high yield often means the price fell because investors doubt the dividend will last.' },
          { q: 'Why do price-only charts understate long-run results for investors?', options: ['They ignore dividends that holders received and could reinvest', 'They are not adjusted for splits', 'They include trading fees', 'They are always measured in euros'], answer: 0, explain: 'Total return counts dividends. Over decades, reinvested dividends make a big difference compared with price alone.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
  ],
  bossQuestions: [
    { q: 'Ortiz Bakeries has 1,000 shares outstanding and Maya owns 20. The company issues 250 new shares to fund an expansion, then later buys back and retires 125 shares. What percentage does Maya own at the end?', options: ['2.0%', '1.6%', '1.78%', '2.22%'], answer: 2, explain: 'After issuing: 1,250 shares, so 20 / 1,250 = 1.6%. After the buyback: 1,125 shares, so 20 / 1,125 = about 1.78%. Issuance dilutes, buybacks concentrate.' },
    { q: 'A company with 100 million shares at $30 does a 3-for-1 split. A month later, nothing else has changed. Which is true?', options: ['Market cap is still about $3 billion, with 300 million shares near $10', 'Market cap is now $9 billion', 'EPS is unchanged at the old level', 'Shareholders own a bigger percentage of the company'], answer: 0, explain: 'Splits change the slicing, not the pie. Shares triple, price divides by three, market cap stays near $3 billion, and EPS is divided by three as well.' },
    { q: 'Lumen Bikes IPOs at $20, pops to $30 on day one, and its 180-day lockup expires next week. Which statement is most accurate?', options: ['Lumen received $30 per share it sold', 'The lockup expiration will force the price back to $20', 'Lumen received about $20 per share before fees, and the coming lockup expiry could add selling pressure', 'Retail investors were guaranteed the $20 IPO price'], answer: 2, explain: 'The company gets the IPO price minus the underwriting spread. Lockup expiry adds potential supply but does not force any particular price.' },
    { q: 'Five years ago a stock had EPS of $2 and a P/E of 25. Today EPS is $3 and the P/E is 20, and it paid no dividends. What was the price change?', options: ['+50%', '+20%', '-20%', '+25%'], answer: 1, explain: 'Old price: $2 x 25 = $50. New price: $3 x 20 = $60. That is +20%. EPS grew 50%, but the multiple shrank from 25 to 20.' },
    { q: 'In a recession with falling interest rates, which pairing describes the textbook tendency?', options: ['Cyclicals like Consumer Discretionary outperform; defensives lag', 'All sectors fall equally because they share one index', 'Energy and Materials always lead', 'Defensive sectors like Staples and Utilities tend to hold up better, and falling rates can help rate-sensitive sectors'], answer: 3, explain: 'Defensives keep selling essentials in downturns, and lower rates ease borrowing costs for debt-heavy, rate-sensitive sectors. These are tendencies, not guarantees.' },
  ],
};
